  "scriptId": "sim-13-set-counting-venn-formula",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Counting with Sets: The Venn Formula",
    "description": "Use n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B) and simple region arithmetic for two- and three-set counting questions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build from the double-counting insight: overlap first, then 'neither' groups, then only-one regions, then three-set peel-off to find the centre, receipt-based reasoning that converts money into ride counts, a full seven-region solve, and a two-step capstone. Every region total is checked against the grand total; each pattern gets a fresh question and a parallel retry.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 26,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 4, Simplification. Approved scope: Teach the inclusion–exclusion idea through the language of the chapter's questions: in a group of 70, 37 like coffee and 52 like tea with everyone liking at least one, so both = 37 + 52 − 70 = 19. Cover all two-set phrasings: finding both, finding neither when some like none, finding only-one counts, and converting between them. Introduce three-set problems with labelled regions (only A, A and B only, all three, and so on), solving for the all-three region from set totals, and receipt-style reasoning such as 85 children, 20 on all three rides, 55 on at least two, total receipt ₹145. Finish with a full small three-set region computation ending in a 'none' count. Learners should tabulate the regions, keep 'both' separate from 'only A', and check that every region count is non-negative and the regions sum to the total."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn to count people who belong to two groups at once.\n\nThe whole lesson is one insight: when you add two groups, the people in both get counted twice. Everything else is bookkeeping.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's count people who belong to two groups at once.\n\nIn a group of 70 people, 37 like coffee, 52 like tea, and each person likes at least one of the two.\n\nHow many like BOTH?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Add the two groups:\n\n37 + 52 = 89\n\nBut there are only 70 people. The extra 19 were counted twice — once in each group.\n\nBoth = 37 + 52 − 70 = 19",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step2": {
      "id": "teach_1_step2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "This is the Venn formula:\n\nn(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B)\n\n∪ means 'in at least one group'.\n∩ means 'in both'.\n\nAdding both groups double-counts the overlap, so subtract it once.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
